[32m+[m[32m## Main Project — Restaurant Robot[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mMy main project is a ROS 2 based restaurant robot.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mWorking inside restaurants has made me familiar with busy kitchen and restaurant environments and the physical demands placed on people working long shifts.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mThat experience strongly influences the kind of robotics problems I want to explore.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mThe restaurant robot is currently my learning and experimentation platform for navigation, robot safety, sensing, decision making, communication between ROS 2 nodes, and simulation.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m### Current Capabilities[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mThe robot can currently:[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32m- move forward and backward[m
[32m+[m[32m- turn left and right[m
[32m+[m[32m- receive obstacle information[m
[32m+[m[32m- make simple navigation decisions[m
[32m+[m[32m- choose a clear direction when obstacles are reported[m
[32m+[m[32m- perform recovery behaviour[m
[32m+[m[32m- enter safe stop conditions[m
[32m+[m[32m- stop safely when an unknown motor command is received[m
[32m+[m[32m- convert navigation decisions into velocity commands[m
[32m+[m[32m- physically move and respond inside Gazebo simulation[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mThe current obstacle information is still produced by simulated ROS 2 sensor publishers.[m
[32m+[m
[32m+[m[32mThe next major step is connecting actual Gazebo sensors such as LiDAR so that obstacles in the simulated environment are detected by the robot itself.[m
